1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,640
Look for evidence that vital-organ perfusion is failing: new
confusion, syncope, weak pulses, cool clammy skin, falling pressure or
significant respiratory distress. A rhythm name and a single pressure
value cannot summarize stability. Establish what changed from the
patient’s baseline.

2
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:40,464
The school lists systolic pressure below ninety as a warning
reference. Other pathways use different numbers for specific
decisions. Do not wait for any cutoff if the patient is clearly
deteriorating; likewise, a currently acceptable pressure does not
erase new altered mentation or ischemic symptoms.

3
00:00:40,464 --> 00:01:00,432
Assess the pattern rather than one sign. Sweating may have several
causes, capillary refill is affected by temperature, and urine output
is rarely measurable immediately in the field. When available, reduced
urine output and delayed refill add context; neither should replace
repeated clinical assessment.

4
00:01:00,432 --> 00:01:19,872
Repeat observations and relate them to symptoms, movement and
interventions. A worsening trend or a mismatch between patient and
monitor should increase concern. Communicate what you actually
measured and what was reported historically rather than presenting all
signs as direct measurements.

5
00:01:19,872 --> 00:01:41,928
Maintain suspicion when symptoms include unexplained dyspnoea, nausea,
sweating, unusual fatigue or discomfort in the jaw, back or arm. Older
adults and people with diabetes may have less prominent pain. These
findings have several possible causes, so assess rather than
automatically diagnosing ACS.

6
00:01:41,928 --> 00:02:00,048
The school calls these atypical presentations. Do not turn that label
into a sex-based stereotype: women also commonly have chest pain, and
people outside the named groups can have pain-free ACS. Use
interpreters and careful questions when communication barriers affect
the history.

7
00:02:00,048 --> 00:02:20,736
For suspected ACS, the linked twenty twenty-five guideline recommends
acquisition and interpretation of a twelve-lead ECG within ten minutes
of first medical contact. Work efficiently without sacrificing
quality, and do not misapply that timing as a reason to postpone
immediate resuscitation.

8
00:02:20,736 --> 00:02:40,656
If the first ECG is nondiagnostic but symptoms persist, clinical
suspicion remains high or the patient worsens, repeated ECG assessment
can reveal change. A normal monitoring lead is not equivalent to a
normal diagnostic twelve-lead, and neither independently excludes ACS.

9
00:02:40,656 --> 00:03:01,992
The school lesson specifies a three-minute pre-notification target
after a qualifying high-risk ECG finding. Retain it as the school’s
performance expectation for practice. The reviewed guideline supports
prompt system activation, but this lesson does not verify the school’s
exact three-minute wording as a universal guideline rule.

10
00:03:01,992 --> 00:03:24,864
Class one describes recommendation strength, not automatically the
highest evidence level. Follow the actual receiving pathway and
medical direction for destination and activation. The school mentions
advanced twelve-lead patterns as a bridge to ECG 301; do not invent
their detailed diagnostic criteria in ECG 101.

11
00:03:24,864 --> 00:03:47,184
An unstable patient with a nonspecific ECG still needs urgent
assessment and authorized support. ACS may be one possibility, but
bleeding, sepsis, pulmonary embolism and other causes can produce
similar deterioration. Do not let the school examples narrow every
shock presentation to coronary disease.

12
00:03:47,184 --> 00:04:09,720
For possible ACS, combine symptom history, serial physiology,
diagnostic ECG and changes over time. Communicate suspected risk and
limitations early. Oxygen, medicines and other interventions follow
indications and the local pathway, not a blanket instruction based
solely on an ECG label.
